<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  PMD ruleset — STUB.

  NAME COLLISION, READ FIRST. This is a PMD ruleset, which is what a bare
  `ruleset.xml` at a repo root means by convention. It is NOT a GitHub
  repository ruleset — those are branch and tag protection rules, they are
  JSON, they are configured in the repository settings UI or via the API, and
  nothing in a file named ruleset.xml has any effect on them. The vault holds
  reference notes on GitHub rulesets ("GitHub Docs - About rulesets.md",
  "Creating rulesets for a repository.md"); this file is unrelated to those and
  changing it will not protect a branch.

  It declares no rules, so a PMD run against it reports nothing. That is the
  intended state: PMD analyzes Java, Apex, and a handful of other languages,
  and this repo's code is Python, shell, and Markdown. There is nothing here
  for PMD to read.

  It exists as the one obvious place to put PMD policy if Java ever arrives —
  and, more usefully today, as the place this note lives so the next person to
  see `ruleset.xml` in a file listing does not mistake it for branch
  protection.

  Shape when this stops being a stub:

    <rule ref="category/java/bestpractices.xml/UnusedPrivateField"/>

    <rule ref="category/java/errorprone.xml">
      <exclude name="MissingSerialVersionUID"/>
    </rule>

  A `<rule ref>` pointing at a whole category file pulls in every rule in it;
  the `<exclude>` form is how you take one back out. Referencing a category and
  then excluding half of it is usually a sign the category was the wrong
  starting point.
-->
<ruleset name="idaho-vault"
         xmlns="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://pmd.sourceforge.net/ruleset/2.0.0 https://pmd.sourceforge.io/ruleset_2_0_0.xsd">

  <description>
    Stub ruleset for IDAHO-VAULT. Declares no rules on purpose — see the
    comment above. Not a GitHub repository ruleset.
  </description>

</ruleset>
